WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:05.840
It’s crazy when you think about all of
the different kinds of foods we eat.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:09.760
We just swallow and hope
it all works out for the best.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:11.280
Well, as it turns out,

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:15.680
there are better ways to think about keeping
our bodies humming healthfully along.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:18.080
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast,

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:20.720
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.360
It’s time for the
NutritionFacts Grab Bag,

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:26.880
where we look at the science
behind a whole array of topics.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:30.400
And we start with how the adverse
effects of industrial pollutants

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:35.440
in seafood may counteract
the benefits of nutrients in fish.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:37.360
Although the levels
of industrial pollutants,

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:39.040
like dioxins and PCBs,

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:41.040
continue to decline
in the food supply,

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.400
there is one dietary source

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:46.160
that still remains
a major threat: fish.

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:48.720
Everything eventually flows
into the sea.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:51.680
Yes, we can get some dioxins
from eating horses,

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:55.200
but most of our exposure
comes from eating fish.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.120
The U.S. Environmental
Protection Agency

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.840
puts the tolerable upper limit
of dioxin intake

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:03.440
per kilogram of body weight
at 0.7 picograms—

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:06.731
less than one trillionth
of a gram— per day.

00:01:06.785 --> 00:01:08.880
We're already skirting the max

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:10.400
by just consuming dairy,

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:13.040
and fish takes us
right over the top,

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:15.600
even at low levels
of consumption.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.160
So, the adverse effects
of chemical contaminants

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:20.480
in seafood may counteract
the benefits

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:22.480
of any nutrients in fish.

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:24.400
So much so
that many dietary guidelines

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.200
recommend no more than
one serving a week of fish

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:29.520
and seafood to cut down
on exposure

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:31.600
to toxic pollutants.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:35.360
But which is worse?
Wild-caught or farmed?

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:37.120
Take salmon for example.

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:40.800
Salmon had the highest toxic
equivalents of PCBs,

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.520
followed by canned tuna,
as well as the highest

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:46.400
neurotoxic equivalents of PCBs.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:48.880
If you compare the levels
of PCBs in salmon

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:50.720
farmed in Maine and Canada,

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:53.280
versus salmon
wild-caught in Alaska,

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:56.960
versus organically farmed salmon
from Norway,

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:58.720
compared
to the wild-caught salmon,

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:01.120
the farmed salmon,
organic or otherwise,

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:05.120
had significantly higher
PCB levels.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:08.160
This appears to extend
to other contaminants too.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:10.240
Based on the testing
of literally tons

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:12.720
of salmon samples
from around the world,

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:15.920
for every toxin
the researchers tested,

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:17.680
the samples of farmed salmon

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:20.960
had higher levels than
did the wild-caught salmon—

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:25.280
higher levels of DDT and
these other banned pesticides.

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:28.640
Over 10 times more PCBs
in farmed salmon;

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:31.440
over 10 times more dioxins.

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:34.160
In order to not exceed
EPA safety levels,

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:36.160
we wouldn't want to eat
supermarket salmon

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:39.840
more than once every two weeks
in Denver or New Orleans,

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:43.200
more than once a month in LA,
Vancouver, DC, Seattle,

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:46.320
Chicago, or New York,
just once every two months

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:50.640
in Edinburgh, Paris,
London, Oslo, Boston,

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:52.640
San Francisco, or Toronto,

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:56.560
and just a few times a year
in Frankfurt, Germany.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:59.600
We can track pollutants
from the ocean to the table,

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:01.040
and it's through the fish oil.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:04.480
The fish oil used in the feed
for farmed fish

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:07.120
transfers pollutants
to the fish themselves,

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:10.160
and then the fish carries the
pollutants to human consumers.

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:13.200
So, yes, salmon has
the healthy omega-3 fatty acids,

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.920
but they also contain
high levels of toxic chemicals,

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:18.720
and not just the PCBs,
dioxins, and pesticides.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:21.840
Farmed salmon test positive
for antibiotic residues,

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:25.120
higher levels of forever
chemicals, and flame retardants,

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:28.960
and also test positive for
endocrine disrupting compounds—

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:30.960
hormone disruptors like BPA.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:33.840
Something you don't see
in samples of sea vegetables

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:37.280
like seaweed, presumably because
of their low fat content.

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:40.000
Farmed salmon may also
have higher levels of mercury

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:41.200
than wild-caught,

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:44.400
though the opposite may be
the case for farmed tuna.

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:46.960
Arsenic also goes both ways,
with more arsenic

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:49.520
in wild seabream
compared to farmed,

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:52.320
but less in wild seabass.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:54.880
However, all the arsenic levels
were bad,

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:56.640
exceeding
cancer benchmark values

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:00.560
indicating a moderate risk of cancer
due to fish consumption.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:03.520
Some analyses have
found dioxin and PCB levels

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:05.920
similar across the board,
but, in general,

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:09.600
you can think of aquaculture
fish as "farmed and dangerous"

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:10.560
compared to wild-caught

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:12.960
in terms of higher
levels of most pollutants.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:15.280
So, that's why researchers
emphasize the importance

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:17.680
of labeling as a means
to help consumers

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:21.360
avoid unnecessary exposure
to highly contaminated fish.

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:24.640
Unfortunately,
mislabeling is rampant.

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:27.440
So, the farmed versus
wild-caught question

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:30.480
may be largely academic
given the extent

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:33.360
of seafood fraud
and mislabeling.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:35.520
An investigation
by the New York Attorney General

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:37.920
found that consumers
who bought what was advertised

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:40.880
as "wild" salmon
actually often received

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:42.720
farm-raised salmon instead.

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:44.800
Up to 43 percent
of salmon tested

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:47.760
across the United States,
for example, were mislabeled,

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:53.120
most commonly passing off
farmed salmon as wild-caught.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:55.040
In our next story,
NutritionFacts.org

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:58.480
Senior Research Scientist
Dr. Kristine Dennis joins us

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:02.080
with a fascinating look at how
turmeric extract supplements

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:04.800
can cause liver injury.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:06.880
Liver injury associated
with turmeric:

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:09.360
Ten cases from the Drug-Induced
Liver Injury Network

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:12.000
in the United States,
and there are others too,

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.480
including three cases
reported in Australia

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:17.360
and a series reported to
the Italian Ministry of Health.

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:20.560
Is turmeric really
a new hepatotoxic substance?

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:24.000
First of all, regardless of what
the titles of the papers say,

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:27.840
these cases appear to be from
turmeric extract supplements,

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:30.400
rather than turmeric the spice.

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:33.680
It's like green tea
and green tea extract pills.

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:35.920
Brewed green tea appears
to be completely safe

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:36.640
for the liver,

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:39.280
but give people green
tea extract supplements,

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:43.920
and the livers of as many as
1 in 17 people becomes inflamed.

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:46.800
So, we should consume
our green tea in beverage form,

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:48.880
not pill form,
and similarly,

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:52.080
our turmeric in spice form
at culinary doses,

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:53.360
not capsules,

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:55.920
unless they contain
just a single ingredient:

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:57.200
whole spice turmeric,

00:05:57.200 --> 00:05:59.360
not curcumin,
not an extract,

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:01.600
no other ingredients.

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:03.200
While turmeric extract
supplements

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:05.440
have a relatively safe
side-effect profile

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:08.160
even at high doses,
there is an increasing number

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:10.800
of case reports linking them
to liver injury,

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:13.440
especially when combined
with agents such as piperine,

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:15.520
a compound found
in black pepper.

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:16.960
As we've covered before,

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:18.960
compounds like piperine
are added to turmeric

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:21.920
to enhance the
bioavailability of curcumin,

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:24.240
potentially contributing
to its toxicity,

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:28.640
as one reason curcumin may be so
safe is that it is poorly absorbed.

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:29.920
As we have cautioned before,

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:32.000
piperine blocks
liver detox enzymes

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:36.080
that can make some substances
like curcumin more potent.

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:38.400
And turmeric curcumin
supplement formulations

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:41.520
with high bioavailability,
such as nanoformulations

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:44.880
or those with added piperine,
have been less investigated.

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:47.680
There is a mistaken assumption
that if a little is good,

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:49.600
then more must be better.

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:51.840
We'd never make
that assumption for drugs,

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:53.760
so why is it made
for supplements?

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:57.120
In this case, more does not
necessarily mean better.

00:06:57.120 --> 00:06:59.840
A review of nearly a hundred
randomized controlled trials

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:01.520
of turmeric or
curcumin supplements

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:03.920
for nonalcoholic
fatty liver disease

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:06.240
found that they successfully
calmed liver inflammation.

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:08.000
And guess
which worked better?

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:10.480
Low-dose supplements
worked about 10 times better

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:11.680
than high-dose ones.

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:14.960
There are caveats, though,
even for whole food turmeric.

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:17.280
As Dr. Greger
has covered before,

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:19.440
people who have a tendency
to form kidney stones

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:20.320
should be careful

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:23.411
due to turmeric's relatively
high oxalate content.

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:26.160
Dr. Greger recommends
only a quarter teaspoon a day

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:27.360
in his Daily Dozen.

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:29.360
There's also been concern
about lead contamination

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:31.360
of the whole food spice.

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:32.640
Lead chromate
has been used

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:35.520
to make turmeric appear
more yellow in Bangladesh,

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:38.800
where much of turmeric sold in
the United States is imported from,

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:41.120
leading to multiple recalls.

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:44.080
The wonderful news is
food safety policies can work!

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:46.640
Since the original paper
of lead chromate contamination

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:48.560
in turmeric from Bangladesh,

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:50.240
the portion
of market turmeric samples

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:51.440
with detectable lead

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:54.960
went from about 50 percent
to zero.

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:57.920
Unfortunately, there is still
more work to be done, though.

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:00.960
In four South Asian countries
where more than 80 percent

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:03.280
of the world's turmeric
is produced,

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:08.160
14 percent of samples had
detectable lead above 2 ug/g.

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:10.000
For context,
this is 20 times higher

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:11.840
than the U.S. Food
and Drug Administration's

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:14.560
maximum allowable level
for lead in candy.

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:16.160
And some stores
sold turmeric

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:18.080
containing much higher levels
of lead

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:20.480
that would result
in projected blood lead levels

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:22.640
in children up to
10 times higher

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:24.560
than the U.S. Centers
for Disease Control's

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:26.880
threshold for concern.

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:29.680
This is a reminder that just
because something is natural

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:31.520
doesn't mean
it can't cause harm.

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:33.920
Turmeric dietary supplements
are big business,

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:36.720
estimated to account
for $69 million in sales

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:38.960
in the United States alone.

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:41.680
Though its popularity does help
put the recent spate

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:44.080
of liver toxicity
cases in context.

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:46.400
More than 10 million people
in the United States

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:48.560
take turmeric
or curcumin supplements.

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:51.360
So, that would be like
one case in a million.

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:54.320
Some people may just be more
susceptible geneticallyâ€”

00:08:54.320 --> 00:08:56.000
7 of the 10 patients
in this case series

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:58.480
had a genetic variant
that's relatively rare

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:00.160
in the general population.

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:03.680
Still, it's safer for
everyone to use the real thing

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:06.400
and not some extract
in some supplement.

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:09.840
And please don't let anyone
inject turmeric or curcumin

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:13.840
into your veins.

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:14.640
Finally today,

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:18.960
we look at how pawpaw
fruits contain neurotoxins

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:22.400
that may cause a
neurodegenerative disease.

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:24.400
Yikes.

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:27.440
The pawpaw
is the largest fruit

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:28.880
native to North America,

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:30.986
weighing up
to two pounds (1 kg).

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:33.120
Pawpaws taste like
a mixture of bananas,

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:34.640
mangos, and pineapples.

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:38.320
They're like a tropical
banana-mango custard,

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:40.960
but found in
temperate forests

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:42.880
across the eastern
United States,

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:45.520
nourishing humankind
for thousands of years

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:48.960
and now complete with
their own pawpaw festivals.

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:51.280
Papayas are
sometimes called pawpaws,

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:53.200
but they're
completely different fruits.

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:56.560
The real pawpaw is
also known as the dog banana,

00:09:56.560 --> 00:09:59.120
false-banana,
Indian banana,

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:01.440
prairie banana,
poor man's banana,

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:05.040
Ozark banana,
Banango (I like that one),

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:08.320
Indiana banana
(has a nice ring to it, too),

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:09.760
and the Kentucky banana.

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:11.520
Pawpaws are my
favorite fruit,

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:14.720
or, should I say,
were my favorite fruit.

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:17.360
One of the reasons
that I moved to rural Virginia

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:19.120
was because the property

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:21.840
had a whole grove
of pawpaw trees.

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:24.560
I was going to be
set for life.

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:27.600
That is, until I ate
a few too many

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:28.800
on the first harvest,

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:32.240
became quite ill,
and actually looked them up.

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:33.680
Pro tip: in the future,

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:38.000
look to see if a food is toxic
before you eat lots of it.

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:40.080
Little did I know,
the pawpaw belonged

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:42.400
to an infamous
family of fruits

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:46.400
that contain three different
types of neurotoxins.

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:48.560
I had actually done
a video, years ago,

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:50.480
warning people
not to eat soursop,

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:52.320
also known as graviola,

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:55.840
but didn't realize
pawpaws were related.

00:10:55.840 --> 00:11:00.560
The family includes
soursop, guanabana, sweetsop,

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:03.200
sugar apple, cherimoya,
and custard-apple,

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:06.320
which are all linked to
a neurodegenerative disease

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:09.600
called progressive
supranuclear palsy,

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:11.920
which is like a
treatment-resistant Parkinson's,

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:15.200
with hallucinations,
widespread brain atrophy,

00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:16.880
and dementia.

00:11:16.880 --> 00:11:18.320
And consumption
of these fruits

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:20.720
is associated with
about 20 times the odds

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:23.760
of having this kind of
atypical Parkinson's syndrome.

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:26.160
Expose rats to even
a fraction of the dose

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:27.360
the people were exposed to,

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:29.760
and they develop the same
kind of brain lesions

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:32.560
you see on autopsy
in human victims.

00:11:32.560 --> 00:11:36.080
And, in several patients who
stopped consuming these fruits,

00:11:36.080 --> 00:11:38.240
the progression
of their condition ceased,

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:41.520
and, in one patient,
the symptoms even disappeared

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:43.920
after a change in diet.

00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:46.640
There is an urgent need
to raise public awareness

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:49.840
about the neurotoxicity
of this family of fruits,

00:11:49.840 --> 00:11:52.000
which are often,
ironically, praised

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:55.360
for their taste
and medicinal values.

00:11:55.360 --> 00:11:57.760
In fact, you can find
dietary supplements

00:11:57.760 --> 00:11:58.800
containing these plants,

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:02.000
promoted for their
purported anticancer effects.

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:06.560
And a soursop extract
can inhibit cancer cell growth

00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:09.440
in a petri dish,
but only at concentrations

00:12:09.440 --> 00:12:12.800
much higher than the amount
that kills off brain cells.

00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:15.440
Even a low
cumulative consumption

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:18.560
may worsen disease severity
and cognitive deficits

00:12:18.560 --> 00:12:21.440
in degenerative
Parkinson's type disease,

00:12:21.440 --> 00:12:24.080
as in 0.2 fruit-years.

00:12:24.080 --> 00:12:27.120
So, that's just one-fifth
of a fruit a day for a year,

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:29.280
or a single fruit a month
for six years,

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:32.320
or just one fruit a year
throughout your entire life.

00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:34.160
The bottom line
is that these fruit

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:35.680
could contribute
to the development

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:38.560
of degenerative
parkinsonism in humans,

00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:40.080
and so, as a precaution,

00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:42.800
more restrictive public health
preventive recommendations

00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:45.920
should be made
regarding their consumption.

00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:49.280
So, okay, consumption
of these tropical fruits

00:12:49.280 --> 00:12:53.040
potentially constitutes
a serious public health problem,

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:55.425
but what about pawpaws?

00:12:55.520 --> 00:12:58.560
So, why aren't
pawpaw eaters getting sick?

00:12:58.560 --> 00:13:01.040
Well, it's only ripe
for a few weeks a year,

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:03.920
so, unlike the tropical fruit
that can be eaten year-round,

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:06.640
people may not have
a lot of exposure to them.

00:13:06.640 --> 00:13:10.560
Fresh pawpaws aren't really
even sold commercially, either,

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:12.800
since they bruise so easily,

00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:15.440
but now the fruit's
available in any season,

00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:17.920
sold as frozen pulp or jam,

00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:19.760
and there are
supplements out there too.

00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:21.280
Not to mention,
if you're like me,

00:13:21.280 --> 00:13:23.600
trees in your backyard.

00:13:23.600 --> 00:13:26.880
When the U.S. Food and Drug
Administration was asked about them,

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:29.760
they noted that pawpaw
has a long history of food use,

00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:34.000
and it didn't have any evidence
that the fruit was unsafe to eat.

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:35.760
You'll hear things like this:

00:13:35.760 --> 00:13:37.200
"Currently,
there have been no reports

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:38.720
of neurogenerative diseases

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:41.360
linked to the consumption
of the pawpaw fruit,"

00:13:41.360 --> 00:13:44.480
until, of course, there are.

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:47.760
"Progressive supranuclear
palsy and pawpaw."

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:49.920
Poor guy had trouble
speaking and walking

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:53.760
as his brain
was atrophying in his skull.

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:56.480
Tragically he actually died
before they figured it out,

00:13:56.480 --> 00:13:59.440
but his wife disclosed
that they owned pawpaw trees,

00:13:59.440 --> 00:14:01.680
and he had been
eating dozens a year.

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:03.440
And when researchers
started looking,

00:14:03.440 --> 00:14:05.440
they indeed seemed
to find a link between

00:14:05.440 --> 00:14:07.440
this neurodegenerative disease

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:10.720
and pawpaw consumption
in the United States.

00:14:10.720 --> 00:14:14.640
So, alas,
no more pawpaws for me.

